I’m approaching this problem space from the other side. We can make the NixOS modules themselves have better hygiene by providing some better tools.

https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/pull/503858 helps all NixOS modules that fall into the “Merged into a config file at runtime” and “Inlined into a config in `/nix/store`" categories, so 152 by your count. It provides them a new function that lets the user pass any freeform option as a secret, for any config file format. Secrets can even be passed automatically through systemd credentials. Here’s an excerpt from the new section of the manual in the PR (I’ve truncated a bit the example to fit here, [link to the full example in the PR](https://github.com/ibizaman/nixpkgs/blob/0e36d72d043fc0261bc2a7b2e712b89de03e4365/nixos/doc/manual/development/settings-options.section.md#using-secrets-in-program-settings), and I now realize the example has some issues, I’ve fixed those here, so I’ll be revisiting the PR :sweat_smile:):

```nix
{ config, pkgs, utils, ... }:
let
  cfg = config.services.foo;

  settingsFormat = pkgs.formats.json { };

  secretsReplacement = utils.genSecretsReplacement {
    inherit (settingsFormat) generator;
    loadCredential = true; # Use systemd LoadCredential mechanism
  } cfg.settings "/run/foo/myconfiguration.json";
in
{
  options.services.foo = {
    settings = lib.mkOption {
      type = lib.types.submodule {
        freeformType = settingsFormat.type;
      };
      default = { };
    };
  };

  config = {
    systemd.services.foo = {
      preStart = secretsReplacement.script;
      serviceConfig.LoadCredential = secretsReplacement.credentials;
    };
  };
}
```
